If your HTTP request and response bodies are showing as `[FILTERED]` in the logs, this is likely due to the default behavior of the Azure SDK or the underlying HTTP client (e.g., `reactor-netty` or `okhttp`) redacting sensitive information for security reasons. To see the full request and response bodies printed in your logs, you need to configure the logging settings to disable this filtering or increase verbosity. Here’s how you can achieve this:

---

### Why This Happens
- The Azure SDK (`com.azure.core.http`) and Spring Boot’s default logging (via SLF4J/Logback) often filter sensitive data like request/response bodies, headers (e.g., `Authorization`), or payloads to prevent logging secrets or large data by default.
- The `[FILTERED]` placeholder indicates that the content is intentionally hidden unless explicitly enabled.

---

### Steps to Show Full Request and Response Bodies

#### 1. Adjust Logging Levels
Ensure you’ve set the appropriate logging levels to capture HTTP details. Add or update these in your `application.properties` (or `application.yml`):

```properties
logging.level.com.azure=DEBUG
logging.level.com.azure.core.http=TRACE
logging.level.reactor.netty.http.client=DEBUG
logging.level.org.springframework.web=DEBUG
```

- `com.azure.core.http=TRACE`: Logs raw HTTP traffic.
- `reactor.netty.http.client=DEBUG`: Logs network-level details if the Azure SDK uses Reactor Netty.

#### 2. Configure Azure SDK to Log Full Bodies
The Azure SDK’s HTTP client might redact bodies by default. To disable this, you can customize the `OpenAIClient` with a logging policy:

```java
import com.azure.ai.openai.OpenAIClient;
import com.azure.ai.openai.OpenAIClientBuilder;
import com.azure.core.credential.TokenCredential;
import com.azure.core.http.HttpPipeline;
import com.azure.core.http.HttpPipelineBuilder;
import com.azure.core.http.policy.HttpLogDetailLevel;
import com.azure.core.http.policy.HttpLogOptions;
import com.azure.identity.ClientSecretCredentialBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;

@Configuration
public class AzureOpenAIConfig {

    @Bean
    public TokenCredential tokenCredential() {
        return new ClientSecretCredentialBuilder()
                .clientId("YOUR_CLIENT_ID")
                .clientSecret("YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET")
                .tenantId("YOUR_TENANT_ID")
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public OpenAIClient openAIClient(TokenCredential tokenCredential) {
        // Configure logging to include bodies
        HttpLogOptions logOptions = new HttpLogOptions()
                .setLogLevel(HttpLogDetailLevel.BODY_AND_HEADERS); // Log full request/response including bodies

        HttpPipeline pipeline = new HttpPipelineBuilder()
                .policies(new AzureIdentityCredentialPolicy(tokenCredential)) // Custom policy if needed
                .httpClient(new NettyAsyncHttpClientBuilder().build())
                .build();

        return new OpenAIClientBuilder()
                .endpoint("https://your-resource-name.openai.azure.com/")
                .pipeline(pipeline) // Use custom pipeline with logging
                .httpLogOptions(logOptions) // Enable detailed logging
                .credential(tokenCredential)
                .buildClient();
    }
}
```

- **`HttpLogDetailLevel.BODY_AND_HEADERS`**: This logs both headers and bodies without filtering.
- **Custom Pipeline**: Optional if you need more control, but the `httpLogOptions` alone should suffice.

#### 3. Update Logback Configuration (Spring Boot)
If you’re using Spring Boot with Logback (default logging framework), the `[FILTERED]` might come from Logback’s redaction. Create or edit `src/main/resources/logback-spring.xml`:

```xml
<configuration>
    <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder>
            <pattern>%d{yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss} %-5level %logger{36} - %msg%n</pattern>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!-- Azure SDK logging -->
    <logger name="com.azure" level="DEBUG" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </logger>
    <logger name="com.azure.core.http" level="TRACE" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </logger>

    <!-- Reactor Netty logging -->
    <logger name="reactor.netty.http.client" level="DEBUG" additivity="false">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </logger>

    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </root>
</configuration>
```

- **No Filtering**: This setup doesn’t apply any filters, so bodies should appear in full.

